/** \page jam-tutorial Jam Tutorial

@section JamTutorialJambase Adding Jam Support

This tutorial will show you how to create a project with a self-contained jam
based build system. Therefore, you do not have to install anything on
the build host, but include all necessary files with your project.
The first thing to do is to add a Jambase and the other required support files
to your project. Most of this has to be in a subdirectory named jam in your
project root. Simply copy the files from here:

- https://pt-framework.svn.sourceforge.net/svnroot/pt-framework/trunk/jam/

This directory includes the implementation of the Jambase and the jam sources
used for bootstrapping. To provide a completely self-contained build system,
we also have to make the jam program available. 

For Windows hosts, we can place a precompiled jam binary in the project root. 
You can download jam.exe here:

- https://pt-framework.svn.sourceforge.net/svnroot/pt-framework/trunk/jam.exe

For Unix hosts we add a simple wrapper shell script named jam.sh to the 
project root:

@code
#!/bin/sh

JAM_SOURCES="./builtins.c ./class.c ./command.c ./compile.c ./constants.c 
             ./debug.c ./execunix.c ./expand.c ./fileunix.c ./filesys.c 
             ./function.c ./glob.c ./hash.c ./hcache.c ./hdrmacro.c ./headers.c
             ./jam.c ./jambase.c ./jamgram.c ./lists.c ./make.c ./make1.c 
             ./md5.c ./mem.c ./modules.c ./native.c ./object.c ./option.c 
             ./output.c ./parse.c ./pathunix.c ./pwd.c ./regexp.c ./rules.c 
             ./scan.c ./search.c ./strings.c ./subst.c ./timestamp.c ./variable.c 
             ./w32_getreg.c ./modules/set.c ./modules/path.c ./modules/regex.c 
             ./modules/property-set.c ./modules/sequence.c ./modules/order.c"

if test ! -f ./jam.bin; then
    echo "Building jam executable version..."
    cd jam/src
    cc -o ../../jam.bin $JAM_SOURCES
    cd ../..
    echo "Built jam.bin"
fi

./jam.bin $*
@endcode

The script bootstraps and runs the jam program. This way, we don't have to include 
all possible jam binaries in our project, but rather build it on demand. You can 
also get the script here:

- https://pt-framework.svn.sourceforge.net/svnroot/pt-framework/trunk/jam.sh

When jam is run, it will look for a file named Jambase in the project root,
that references the actual Jambase. For self-contained projects, it will 
simply include the Jambase from the jam subdirectory:

@code
JAMDIR  ?= jam ;
JAMBASE ?= Jambase ;
JAMBASE = $(JAMBASE:R=$(JAMDIR)) ;

include $(JAMBASE) ;
@endcode

Your project directory should now contain the following files:

@verbatim
- [jam]
    - Jambase
    - ... (jam implementation files)
- Jambase
- jam.sh
- jam.exe
@endverbatim

@section JamTutorialConfigure Build Configuration

Now we have to take care of the build configuration. Before a project can be
built, it needs to be configured, so the toolset is detected etc... The 
input for this 'configure' step is a file named Jamconfigure located in the 
project root:

@code
# configure basic C compiler
ConfigureCc ;

# configure basic C++ compiler
ConfigureC++ ;
@endcode

So far, we only need to configure basic C and C++ support. This allows us to
build programs, static libraries and shared libraries using standard C and 
C++. You should be able to run the configure step now sucessfully.

@verbatim
On Unix:
  ./jam.sh configure

On Windows:
  jam.exe configure
@endverbatim

@section JamTutorialBuildPrograms Building Programs 

Let's use jam now to build a simple 'hello world' program. When jam is run
to build its targets in a so-called 'build' step, it looks for a file named
Jamfile in the project root. Here we can define our build targets:

@code
SubDir MY_TOP ;
Main hello : main.cpp ;
@endcode

This Jamfile will build a main program named 'hello' from the source file
main.cpp. The first statement also defines the so-called root variable.
The SubDir rules makes all following statements refer to a specific directory. 
Thus, main.cpp will be looked for in the project root. If you run jam now 
(either the wrapper script or the windows binary without any arguments) a
program will be built named 'hello' on Unix or 'hello.exe' on windows, 
respectively. 

@section JamTutorialClean Cleaning the Build

Jam can not only build programs, but also delete all previously built targets
from your project directory. To do so you need to execute jam with the clean 
target:

@verbatim
On Unix:
  ./jam.sh clean

On Windows:
  jam.exe clean
@endverbatim

*/
